<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <NeutralLanguage>en-US</NeutralLanguage>
    <PackageId>Cynicszm.FlatFiles</PackageId>
    <Authors>Cynicszm</Authors>
    <Description>Reads and writes CSV, fixed-length and other flat file formats with a focus on schema definition, configuration and speed. Supports mapping directly between files and classes. A fork of FlatFiles by Travis Parks, continued for .NET 10. The assembly and namespaces are unchanged, and through 7.6.0 every type and member name was too, so only the package reference differed; 8.0.0 renames a few of them, each named in the changelog.</Description>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Cynicszm/FlatFiles</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Cynicszm/FlatFiles.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>csv;comma;tab;separated;value;delimited;flat;file;fixed;width;fixed-width;length;fixed-length;parser;parsing;parse</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>9.2.1 - A type mapper defined a dynamic assembly every time it was asked to read or write. Read, Write, GetReader and GetWriter each built a fresh mapper, and with it a fresh code generator, which defines a dynamic assembly of its own; the emitted serialiser was cached on the mapper that was being thrown away, so reusing the type mapper did not help. A caller writing one record at a time built one dynamic assembly per record, until the process ran out of mappings for executable memory and the runtime failed with 'Failed to create RW mapping for RX memory' - a message naming insufficient memory and a Linux kernel limit, neither of which is the cause. The mapper and its generator are now held and reused. Turning optimisation off after a write still stops it emitting, and adding a mapping after a write still takes effect. No API changes.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <SignAssembly>true</SignAssembly>
    <AssemblyOriginatorKeyFile>FlatFiles.snk</AssemblyOriginatorKeyFile>
    <Version>9.2.1</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Ships with the package: IntelliSense text, and a symbol package whose Source Link points at the commit. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <!-- Pack fails if the public API loses or changes a member relative to the last release, so a breaking
         change has to be declared here by moving the baseline, not discovered by a consumer. -->
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
    <PackageValidationBaselineVersion>9.2.0</PackageValidationBaselineVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyVersion>9.2.1.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>9.2.1.0</FileVersion>
    <PackageLicenseFile>UNLICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Update="Properties\Resources.Designer.cs">
      <DesignTime>True</DesignTime>
      <AutoGen>True</AutoGen>
      <DependentUpon>Resources.resx</DependentUpon>
    </Compile>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Update="Properties\Resources.resx">
      <Generator>ResXFileCodeGenerator</Generator>
      <LastGenOutput>Resources.Designer.cs</LastGenOutput>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\icon.png">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath></PackagePath>
    </None>
    <None Include="..\UNLICENSE.txt">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath></PackagePath>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The generator is built before this package is packed, but nothing in the library refers to it:
         it runs inside the compiler, not beside the library. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FlatFiles.Generator\FlatFiles.Generator.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer" />
    <None Include="..\FlatFiles.Generator\bin\$(Configuration)\netstandard2.0\FlatFiles.Generator.dll"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs"
          Visible="false" />
    <!-- Declares FlatFilesGenerateAccessors to the compiler, so the generator can be told to write nothing.
         build applies to a project that references this package; buildTransitive to one that gets it through
         another package. -->
    <None Include="build\Cynicszm.FlatFiles.props"
          Pack="true"
          PackagePath="build;buildTransitive" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
